Testing [6. Face Attendance]: "how do I start face attendance?"
Reply: Here’s how you can start a face‑attendance session in the Student Management System:

1. **Open the Attendance Dashboard**  
   - From the faculty sidebar, click **“Attendance”** (or go to `/attendance` if you’re using a direct URL).

2. **Select the Course and Session**  
   - In the top panel, choose the course you want to take attendance for (e.g., *CS101 – Introduction to Programming*).  
   - Pick the date and session time (the system will auto‑populate the next available slot if you’re running a live class).

3. **Choose the “Face Attendance” Mode**  
   - On the right‑hand side of the session screen, you’ll see three options:  
     - **Manual Log** (toggle each student’s status)  
     - **QR Code** (display a dynamic QR for students to scan)  
     - **Face Attendance** (start facial recognition).  
   - Click the **Face Attendance** button.

4. **Set Up the Camera**  
   - The system will request access to your device’s camera. Allow it, and position the camera so all students in the classroom can be seen.  
   - If you’re using a webcam or a classroom projector, make sure the image is clear and well‑lit.

5. **Start the Session**  
   - Click **“Start Attendance”**.  
   - A countdown timer will appear (default is the session duration you selected).  
   - As students enter the classroom, the system will automatically detect faces, log them as “Present,” and update the attendance sheet in real time.

6. **Monitor Live Status**  
   - The attendance sheet will update instantly. If a student’s face isn’t detected, they’ll remain marked as “Absent” until you manually change it or they appear later.

7. **Finish & Save**  
   - When the session ends, click **“End Session”**.  
   - The attendance data will be saved automatically.  
   - The system will also check the 75 % compliance rule and flag any students falling below that threshold.

8. **Optional: Manual Adjustments**  
   - After the session, you can still edit individual student statuses (e.g., mark a student as “Late” or “On Leave”) before finalizing.

That’s it! You’ve now started a face‑attendance session. If you run into any camera‑related issues or if the system fails to recognize certain faces, double‑check lighting and camera angles, and consider re‑capturing the session. Happy teaching!

